"A change-data-capture diagram explains how committed database changes become downstream change events.\n\nUse when: Review database-to-stream replication and incremental ingestion. Explain snapshot-to-log handoff and consumer recovery.\n\nAvoid when: The source emits domain events directly rather than captured database changes.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes distinguish source database, transaction log, connector, broker, and sink. Arrows carry snapshot rows or change events as separately labeled paths. Offset and checkpoint annotations identify recoverable progress.\n\nBuild: Choose the source engine and connector implementation. Draw initial snapshot and ongoing log paths separately. Mark ordering keys, checkpoints, and schema-change handling. Place recovery notes outside the main data path and reserve clear connector corridors.\n\nCheck: Captured changes are not mislabeled as business-domain events. Snapshot consistency and handoff assumptions are explicit. Deletes, tombstones, and replay behavior are documented where relevant. Offset labels and recovery arrows remain separate from node text.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming a connector sees changes before transaction commit. Treating offsets as proof that every downstream effect completed exactly once.\n\nExample: An Orders database snapshot seeds a topic, after which log-based changes update a search index.\n\nAlternatives: Transactional outbox diagram, ETL pipeline diagram."
